        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "Toronto's offseason isn't defined by what slipped away last spring - 78 points, eighth in the Atlantic, and a season that ended quietly - but by the sharp, deliberate moves reshaping the roster now. The Maple Leafs don't have cap space, yet they wield flexibility, and that paradox is driving their most concrete action: <strong>Jacob Quillan reportedly signs a one-year, $850K extension with the Maple Leafs</strong>, locking in a trusted depth piece while the front office hunts for a difference-maker. With Friedman noting the team remains hopeful to add one more pivotal player, the focus shifts to cap engineering and strategic signings rather than panic. The Leafs are building a leaner, more agile unit, betting on internal growth and smart acquisitions. <strong>What to watch next is whether they can squeeze out a high-impact free agent without triggering a cap crisis</strong>, because that decision will define their 2026-27 trajectory.",
        "updated": "Updated July 7, 2026"
      },
